# The ledger of `bench`: what a program can do to measure a function, one
# block per capability, and what Rebel answers. The lists are this machine's,
# 2026-11-19, read and never their source: Go's `testing` (`go doc -all`,
# Go 1.27.3: `B`, `BenchmarkResult`, `PB`, `Benchmark`, `AllocsPerRun`);
# Rust's standard library (`std::hint::black_box`) and its unstable `test`
# crate (`test::bench` and `test::stats`, read from the documentation under
# the toolchain), and `criterion` 0.8.2, fetched into the scratch folder and
# read from `cargo doc` with every method of `Criterion`, `Bencher`,
# `BenchmarkGroup` and their enums; Python's `timeit` and `pyperf` 2.11.0,
# installed into the scratch folder and read by `dir()`.
#
# The rules every block keeps are the project's own, `tests/harness/
# latency_clock.hero` and `docs/measurements/061-the-latency-ledger.md`: the
# first run is discarded, a run whose wall time stands well above its
# processor time is discarded as waiting, the best of what is kept is the
# number and the worst less the best its spread; two versions are compared
# only in pairs alternated in one sitting, read by the sign test, a change
# under a floor of effect counted none, never by two results of two sittings.

capability bench/measure  a function called many times and its time a call told
  heroes  bench.measure
  go      testing.Benchmark, testing.B, B.N, B.Loop, B.Elapsed, BenchmarkResult, BenchmarkResult.N, BenchmarkResult.T, BenchmarkResult.NsPerOp
  rust    criterion::Criterion::bench_function, criterion::Bencher::iter, test::bench::Bencher::iter, test::bench::benchmark, test::bench::iter, test::bench::run_once
  python  timeit.timeit, timeit.Timer, timeit.Timer.timeit, pyperf.Runner, pyperf.Runner.bench_func
  note    the time a call is a round's wall time over its calls; the median is told first and the best is the project's number (the best of the rounds kept, the first discarded), where Go reports the mean of one run of `N` and criterion a bootstrapped estimate

capability bench/input  the function measured on an input the program gives
  heroes  bench.measure
  go      -
  rust    criterion::Criterion::bench_with_input, criterion::BenchmarkGroup::bench_with_input, criterion::BenchmarkId, BenchmarkId::new, BenchmarkId::from_parameter
  python  pyperf.Runner.bench_func
  note    the input is a parameter, since a function value carries no state; a work of several inputs takes a record

capability bench/warm-up  rounds run and discarded before the rounds that count
  heroes  bench.with_warmups
  go      -
  rust    criterion::Criterion::warm_up_time, criterion::BenchmarkGroup::warm_up_time
  python  pyperf.Run.warmups
  note    rounds, not a time as criterion's: one by default, as pyperf's `Runner` (`warmups=1`, read 2026-11-19) and the project's first discarded, after the rounds that settle the calls, which are discarded too

capability bench/rounds  how many rounds are measured
  heroes  bench.with_rounds
  go      -
  rust    criterion::Criterion::sample_size, criterion::BenchmarkGroup::sample_size, test::bench::BenchSamples
  python  timeit.repeat, timeit.Timer.repeat, timeit.default_repeat, pyperf.Benchmark.get_nrun, pyperf.Benchmark.get_nvalue, pyperf.Run.values, pyperf.Benchmark.get_runs, pyperf.Benchmark.get_values
  note    20 by default

capability bench/time  measuring stopped after a time
  heroes  bench.with_time
  go      -
  rust    criterion::Criterion::measurement_time, criterion::BenchmarkGroup::measurement_time
  python  pyperf.Benchmark.get_total_duration
  note    counted from the start of the measurement; it stops at the first round ending past it, and at least one round runs after the warm-ups

capability bench/calls-per-round  each round repeating the call until it lasts long enough for the clock
  heroes  bench.with_round
  go      B.N
  rust    criterion::SamplingMode, SamplingMode::Auto, SamplingMode::Linear, SamplingMode::Flat, criterion::BenchmarkGroup::sampling_mode
  python  timeit.Timer.autorange, timeit.default_number, pyperf.Benchmark.get_loops, pyperf.Benchmark.get_inner_loops, pyperf.Benchmark.get_total_loops, pyperf.Run.get_loops, pyperf.Run.get_inner_loops, pyperf.Run.get_total_loops
  note    a round repeats the call until it lasts `round`, 100 milliseconds by default, pyperf's `min_time` of 0.1 s (read 2026-11-19); the calls are settled as Go's `testing.B` settles `N`, each try predicting the calls that reach it and a fifth more, at most a hundredfold, at most a billion

capability bench/waiting  a round that waited on something other than its work discarded
  heroes  bench.with_waiting
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the project's rule (`tests/harness/latency_clock.hero`), none of the three libraries': a round whose wall time stands above the process's processor time by more than a share of it (a tenth by default) and more than 20 milliseconds, the step Windows counts processor time in, is discarded; a work that waits by nature keeps every round with `math.INF`

capability bench/rounds-told  every round with its calls, its wall and processor time, and what became of it
  heroes  bench.Report, bench.Round
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  pyperf.Benchmark.get_runs, pyperf.Run
  note    every round with its calls, its wall and processor time and the reason it was kept or discarded

capability bench/summary  the median, mean, best, worst, deviations and quartiles of the times
  heroes  bench.summarize, bench.Summary
  go      -
  rust    test::stats::Stats, Stats::min, Stats::max, Stats::mean, Stats::median, Stats::var, Stats::std_dev, Stats::std_dev_pct, Stats::median_abs_dev, Stats::median_abs_dev_pct, Stats::quartiles, Stats::iqr, Stats::sum, Stats::percentile, test::stats::Summary, Summary::new, test::bench::BenchSamples::ns_iter_summ
  python  pyperf.Benchmark.mean, pyperf.Benchmark.median, pyperf.Benchmark.stdev, pyperf.Benchmark.median_abs_dev, pyperf.Benchmark.percentile
  note    `stats`'s median, mean and deviation answer Python's `statistics`; the quartiles are the inclusive ones, as criterion's fences read; the median deviation is pyperf's and Rust's `median_abs_dev` unscaled

capability bench/outliers  the rounds far from the others, counted by Tukey's fences
  heroes  bench.Outliers
  go      -
  rust    test::stats::winsorize
  python  -
  note    criterion's classification by Tukey's fences, 1.5 and 3 interquartile ranges; the outliers are counted and kept, never clipped as Rust's `winsorize` clips, the median and the best not moving with them

capability bench/compare  two versions measured in pairs and the change judged against a floor
  heroes  bench.compare_versions, bench.with_floor
  go      -
  rust    criterion::Criterion::noise_threshold, significance_level, confidence_level, nresamples, criterion::BenchmarkGroup::noise_threshold, significance_level, confidence_level, nresamples
  python  -
  note    the project's paired reading: rounds of the two run in pairs alternately and in turns of order, the first pair discarded, pairs that waited dropped, the pairs read by the sign test one-sided in the way most fell at 0.05 over at least 5 untied pairs, a change within the floor of effect (0.5 % by default, decision 2282's) counted none; criterion's bootstrap with a noise threshold reads two separate samples, which the project measured as missing a regression its pairs saw (the latency ledger, 2026-10-31)

capability bench/compare-pairs  timings the program made itself in pairs, compared
  heroes  bench.compare_pairs
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the same reading of pairs the program timed itself

capability bench/own-timing  the program timing its own work, setup left out, and the times summarized
  heroes  bench.summarize, bench.compare_pairs
  go      B.ResetTimer, B.StartTimer, B.StopTimer
  rust    criterion::Bencher::iter_custom, criterion::Bencher::iter_batched, criterion::Bencher::iter_batched_ref, criterion::Bencher::iter_with_large_drop, criterion::BatchSize, BatchSize::SmallInput, BatchSize::LargeInput, BatchSize::PerIteration, BatchSize::NumBatches, BatchSize::NumIterations
  python  pyperf.Runner.bench_time_func, pyperf.Runner.timeit, pyperf.perf_counter, timeit.default_timer
  note    a program that leaves a setup out times its own work with `time.mark` and `time.elapsed` and hands the times to `summarize`, which is what `StopTimer` and `iter_custom` are for

capability bench/throughput  bytes or elements a second
  heroes  bench.throughput
  go      B.SetBytes, BenchmarkResult.Bytes
  rust    criterion::Throughput, Throughput::Bytes, Throughput::BytesDecimal, Throughput::Bits, Throughput::Elements, Throughput::ElementsAndBytes, criterion::BenchmarkGroup::throughput, test::bench::BenchSamples::mb_s, test::bench::Bencher::bytes
  python  -
  note    units a second at the median: bytes, elements or bits as the program counts them

capability bench/format  a time written for a reader in its unit
  heroes  bench.format_nanoseconds
  go      BenchmarkResult.String
  rust    test::bench::fmt_bench_samples
  python  pyperf.Benchmark.format_value, pyperf.Benchmark.format_values, pyperf.Benchmark.get_unit
  note    three figures and the unit that keeps them at 1 or above, `µs` as Go and criterion write it

capability bench/report  a run told in one line
  heroes  bench.Report
  go      BenchmarkResult.String
  rust    -
  python  -

capability bench/black-box  the result of the work kept from the compiler, so the call is not removed
  heroes  bench.measure
  go      -
  rust    std::hint::black_box, criterion::black_box, test::black_box, test::bench::black_box
  python  -
  note    the work is called through a function value and its answer dropped; measured on 2026-11-19, a hundred million calls of a function adding to its argument took 4.1 ns each, the call not removed, so no black box is needed

capability bench/allocations  the allocations a call makes, counted
  heroes  refused: the runtime counts no allocation a program can read
  go      B.ReportAllocs, testing.AllocsPerRun, BenchmarkResult.MemAllocs, BenchmarkResult.MemBytes, BenchmarkResult.AllocsPerOp, BenchmarkResult.AllocedBytesPerOp, BenchmarkResult.MemString
  rust    -
  python  -

capability bench/metrics  a measure of the program's own reported beside the time, or another clock
  heroes  refused: a figure of the program's own is the program's to compute and print beside `Report.text`; the processor time of every round is told in `Round.processor_ns`, the one other clock
  go      B.ReportMetric, BenchmarkResult.Extra
  rust    criterion::measurement::Measurement, criterion::measurement::WallTime, criterion::measurement::ValueFormatter, criterion::Criterion::with_measurement
  python  -

capability bench/parallel  the work run on many threads at once
  heroes  refused: a thread of the language runs a function of one `i64` or one `str` (spec § 13), so a work of any type cannot be run on many threads by this package
  go      B.RunParallel, B.SetParallelism, PB, PB.Next
  rust    -
  python  -

capability bench/groups  benchmarks named and grouped, a filter choosing which run
  heroes  refused: a measurement is a call the program names, a group its own list of reports, and a filter its own `if`
  go      B.Run, B.Name
  rust    criterion::Criterion::benchmark_group, criterion::BenchmarkGroup, BenchmarkGroup::bench_function, BenchmarkGroup::finish, criterion::Criterion::with_filter, criterion::Criterion::with_benchmark_filter, criterion::BenchmarkFilter, criterion::criterion_group!, criterion::criterion_main!
  python  pyperf.BenchmarkSuite, BenchmarkSuite.add_benchmark, BenchmarkSuite.get_benchmark, BenchmarkSuite.get_benchmark_names, BenchmarkSuite.get_benchmarks, pyperf.Benchmark.get_name

capability bench/baselines  results saved and compared with a later sitting's
  heroes  refused: two sittings are two dates, not a measurement (`docs/measurements/061-the-latency-ledger.md`); two versions are compared by `compare_versions` in one sitting
  go      -
  rust    criterion::Criterion::save_baseline, criterion::Criterion::retain_baseline, criterion::Baseline, Baseline::CompareLenient, Baseline::CompareStrict, Baseline::Discard, Baseline::Save
  python  pyperf.Benchmark.dump, pyperf.Benchmark.load, pyperf.Benchmark.loads, pyperf.Benchmark.add_run, pyperf.Benchmark.add_runs, pyperf.add_runs, pyperf.BenchmarkSuite.dump, pyperf.BenchmarkSuite.load, pyperf.BenchmarkSuite.loads, pyperf.BenchmarkSuite.add_runs

capability bench/plots  the times drawn
  heroes  refused: Rebel draws no picture of a measurement; the times are values a program may write out
  go      -
  rust    criterion::Criterion::with_plots, criterion::Criterion::without_plots, criterion::Criterion::plotting_backend, criterion::PlottingBackend, criterion::AxisScale, criterion::PlotConfiguration, criterion::BenchmarkGroup::plot_config
  python  -

capability bench/processes  each run in a fresh process, the machine tuned
  heroes  refused: a process for each run isolates an interpreter's warm state, which a program compiled ahead of time has none of; a whole program is measured by `process` and `time`
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  pyperf.Runner.bench_command, pyperf.Runner.is_worker, pyperf.Benchmark.required_nprocesses

capability bench/metadata  the machine and the sitting recorded beside the times
  heroes  refused: the machine is `os`'s to tell (`os.system`, `os.processor_count`, `os.load_average`), which a program prints beside its report
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  pyperf.Benchmark.get_metadata, pyperf.Benchmark.update_metadata, pyperf.BenchmarkSuite.get_metadata, pyperf.format_metadata, pyperf.Benchmark.get_dates, pyperf.BenchmarkSuite.get_dates, pyperf.python_implementation, pyperf.python_has_jit, pyperf.VERSION

dropped plumbing  a test runner's machinery and the parts of a library's insides a program does not call
  go      B.Attr, B.ArtifactDir, B.Chdir, B.Cleanup, B.Context, B.Error, B.Errorf, B.Fail, B.FailNow, B.Failed, B.Fatal, B.Fatalf, B.Helper, B.Log, B.Logf, B.Output, B.Setenv, B.Skip, B.SkipNow, B.Skipf, B.Skipped, B.TempDir (what `B` shares with `T`, the test runner's; a Heroes test is a `test` block)
  rust    criterion::Criterion::configure_from_args, criterion::Criterion::with_output_color, criterion::Criterion::with_profiler, criterion::profiler::Profiler, criterion::profiler::ExternalProfiler, criterion::async_executor::AsyncExecutor, criterion::Criterion::init, criterion::Criterion::default (a command line, colours, a profiler hook and an async executor, which a program here has none of)
  python  pyperf.Runner.parse_args, pyperf.Runner.bench_async_func, timeit.main, timeit.template, timeit.reindent, timeit.dummy_src_name, timeit.Timer.print_exc, timeit.default_target_time (a command line, coroutines, and timeit's code given as text, which Heroes runs as a function)
